1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,312
What Causes Earthquakes?

2
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:11,550
Earthquakes result from the sudden release of elastic strain energy stored in the Earth's crust, most commonly along fault lines where tectonic plates interact.

3
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:22,625
The lithosphere is divided into approximately 15 major tectonic plates that move relative to each other at rates of 2-15 centimeters per year, driven by mantle convection.

4
00:00:22,625 --> 00:00:40,937
The elastic rebound theory, formulated by Harry Fielding Reid following the 1906 San Francisco earthquake, describes the process: tectonic forces gradually deform rock near a fault until accumulated stress exceeds frictional strength, causing sudden slip and the radiation of seismic waves.

5
00:00:40,937 --> 00:00:43,775
Three primary fault types exist.

6
00:00:43,775 --> 00:00:50,675
Strike-slip faults, exemplified by the San Andreas Fault in California, accommodate lateral motion.

7
00:00:50,675 --> 00:00:55,337
Normal faults occur in extensional settings such as the East African Rift.

8
00:00:55,337 --> 00:01:03,062
Reverse or thrust faults, found at subduction zones where one plate dives beneath another, generate the largest earthquakes.

9
00:01:03,062 --> 00:01:18,112
The 2004 Sumatra-Andaman earthquake (Mw 9.1) occurred on a megathrust fault where the Indian plate subducts beneath the Burma plate, producing a tsunami that killed approximately 230,000 people across 14 countries.

10
00:01:18,112 --> 00:01:29,775
Earthquake magnitude is measured using the moment magnitude scale (Mw), which estimates energy release based on fault rupture area, slip distance, and the rigidity of surrounding rock.

11
00:01:29,775 --> 00:01:36,962
Each unit increase on this logarithmic scale corresponds to roughly 31.6 times more energy release.

12
00:01:36,962 --> 00:01:48,412
The largest instrumentally recorded earthquake was the 1960 Valdivia earthquake in Chile (Mw 9.5), with a fault rupture extending over 1,000 kilometers.

13
00:01:48,412 --> 00:02:02,912
Seismic waves propagate as body waves — compressional P-waves and slower shear S-waves — and surface waves (Love and Rayleigh waves), with surface waves causing the most structural damage due to their larger amplitudes and lower frequencies.

14
00:02:02,912 --> 00:02:13,937
The Pacific Ring of Fire, a 40,000-kilometer horseshoe-shaped zone, hosts approximately 90% of the world's earthquakes due to extensive plate subduction along its perimeter.

